#import "template.typ": *

#let metadata = (
  title: "Low Level Navigation, EFB, Consolidation, Solo assessment",
  unit: "Cross Country Endorsement | Lesson 6",
  author: "C.Moroney",
  version: "v1.0.0",
)

#let lesson = (
title: [#metadata.title]

,aim: [
To navigate safely at low level to a nominated destination by dead reckoning on ground-based features, and to consolidate the use of the EFB/GPS as a supplement to navigation — including operating it in a limited (degraded) mode and reverting to DR, assess navigation standard in preparation for solo flight.
]


,overview: [
This lesson addresses element *1.4 Low level navigation* (navigate at low level, identify LSALT, identify and locate hazards, dead-reckon by ground-based features, configure the aircraft for appropriate performance) and consolidates *1.8 Use of electronic devices* by exercising the EFB/GPS in both *operational and limited modes* — the specific competency variable. Low-level diversions are an efficient way to practise both diversion and low-level skills together. The route may be complex; it should include at least one leg suitable for low-level navigation between two prominent towns, landmarks or aerodromes. The student should be able to successfully navigate the standard portion, including an arrival and TGL at a foreign airport and conduct a normal diversion at solo standard. Then, the low level portion will be conducted with instructor assistance initially, with the student demonstrating solo standard later in the flight.
]

,learning_outcomes: [
#text(size:12pt)[*Knowledge*]
- State the reasons low-level flight may be necessary and identify its risks.
- Identify and calculate/confirm LSALT for a route segment.
- Explain navigation by ground-based features at 500 ft AGL (and how the technique changes from normal altitudes).
- Explain how electronic devices determine position; the limitations of non-TSO devices; triangulation/position error on signal loss; EFB redundancy and regulatory requirements; mounting/storage provisions.
#text(size:12pt)[*Skills*]
- Navigate safely at low level to a nominated destination, following a feature (road, rail, river, powerline).
- Configure the aircraft appropriately for low-level performance (speed/flap as required).
- Operate the EFB in operational mode and, on instructor command, in a limited mode (simulated signal loss/degraded) — reverting to DR/pilotage.
- Conduct a low-level diversion.
#text(size:12pt)[*Non-Technical Skills*]
- [TEM] Maintain a continuous "Plan B": where to go if the valley/route ahead is blocked.
- [HF] Manage heightened workload and reduced margins at low level.
- [SA] Maintain SA on terrain, obstacles and rising ground.
- [TEM] Apply ADM: do not continue if weather/terrain margins erode.
]

,theory_knowledge: [
- Meteorological hazards — fog, thunderstorms, gusts/squalls, microbursts, dust (Pg 262–266)
- Lost procedure *(Pg 259)*
- Fatigue and body rhythms *(Pg 39–40)*
- v8 Theory Unit 2.03 §1.1.7 (map reading at low level — 500 ft AGL, over mountainous/coastal/settled/sparsely-populated terrain) and §1.1.8 (EFB/GPS: position determination, limitations, legislative and backup requirements, mounting/storage).
]

,ground_brief_topics: [
Total time: 55 mins \
Equipment required: VNC/WAC for the route, ERSA, plotter, E6B, EFB, nav log, NAIPS access, current GAF/GPWT and NOTAM \

*Non-Technical Skills* (5 minutes)
- [TEM] Plan B at all times; weather/terrain decision points.
- [HF] Workload and reduced margins at low level.
- [SA] Terrain, obstacles and rising ground.

*Why low level, and its risks* (10 minutes)
- Reasons for low level; hazards; precautionary search and landing always available.

*LSALT and configuration* (10 minutes)
- Identifying/calculating LSALT; minimum 500 ft AGL; slowing the aircraft and use of flap as required.

*Low-level navigation technique* (15 minutes)
- Navigate by ground features; follow a feature to the destination; navigate away from high terrain; keep position on the chart at all times.

*EFB operational vs limited mode* (15 minutes)
- How devices determine position; non-TSO limitations; triangulation/position error on signal loss; redundancy/regulatory requirements; mounting and storage. Plan to demonstrate a simulated signal loss and reversion to DR (v8 §1.8 competency variable).
]

,ground_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor*]
- Review weather, NOTAM and fuel; select a route with a suitable low-level leg between prominent features.
- Confirm LSALT and terrain/obstacle hazards for the low-level segment.
- Plan where to introduce a simulated EFB signal loss/limited mode.
#text(size:12pt)[*Student*]
- Complete IMSAFE; brief the go/no-go decision.
- Prepare a complete nav log (TR, HDG, ETI, FUEL, frequencies); identify LSALT and hazards for the low-level leg.
- Submit a flight plan to NAIPS.
]

,in_flight_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Demonstration*]
- A simulated EFB signal loss/limited mode and reversion to DR/pilotage.
#text(size:12pt)[*Student Practice*]
- Navigate the aircraft to solo standard following a pre-planned route, and perform a normal (high altitude) diversion.
- Navigate the route largely unassisted; instructor assists keeping up with the chart at low level.
- Fly one leg at low level, configuring the aircraft appropriately.
- Identify which way to turn and which feature to follow; answer "what's Plan B?" prompts.
- Operate the EFB in operational mode, then in limited mode on command, reverting to DR.
- Conduct a low-level diversion.
- Maintain a continuous fuel log and forced-landing awareness.
]

,common_errors:[
- *Descending below 500 ft AGL or climbing*: 500 feet AGL is the legal minimum. Most low level diversions can be performed at ~800 feet AGL.
- *Fixating on the feature*: Keep scanning for terrain, obstacles and traffic, not just the line you're following.
- *No CLEAROFF during low level*: CLEAROFF discipline must be maintained during low-level navigation.
]

,post_flight:[
- *Student Self-Assessment*: What went well; how workload and margins felt at low level.
- *Instructor Feedback* (10 min):
  - Evaluate against the standards below (S / C / NC).
  - Highlight strengths; nominate focus areas.
  - Discuss TEM/ADM (eg. terrain decision points; EFB reversion).
- *Scenario Reflection*: "Cloud lowers ahead while you're at 700 ft following a valley — what are your options and triggers?"
- *Next Steps*: Assign homework. See below.
]

,standards_for_progression:[
- Uses 1-in-60 effectively; revises ETA (when required) to within ±3 minutes.
- Position-fixes quickly and accurately, including at low level.
- Maintains straight-and-level ±100 ft and heading ±5° at normal altitudes.
- Operates the nav log on standard flights and diversions; manages fuel log and frequencies; recalls/implements CLEAROFF.
- Arrives at and conducts a touch-and-go at a foreign aerodrome without assistance.
- Conducts a low-level diversion effectively and safely; identifies LSALT and hazards.
- Operates the EFB in both operational and limited modes, reverting to DR.
- *Competency stage*: this lesson all required lessons — next lesson will assess all elements toward *S (Solo standard)*. Record S / P / C / NC.
]

,safety:[
- *Weather*: Visible horizon; good visibility; low-level segment requires clearly suitable conditions — abandon or stay high if marginal.
- *Altitude*: En route minimum 1,500 ft AGL. Low-level segment no lower than 500 ft AGL, known terrain only. Avoid overflying houses, do not overfly townships.
- *Emergency Procedures*: Continuous forced-landing options; precautionary search and landing briefed and available; defined turn-back/divert triggers.
- *TEM*:
  - Manage reduced margins and higher workload at low level; instructor maintains a low intervention threshold.
  - Take control if terrain, obstacle or weather margins erode.
  - Do not overfly sensitive features (eg. dam walls) — divert around as briefed.
  - Monitor traffic and birds at low level.
- *Suggested Routes*: YHEC-YCHB (overfly)- Divert to Killarney - Low level to Woodenbong - YHEC | YHEC-GON-DBO-YWSG (TGL)- Low level to Lake Samsonvale-GON-YHEC
- *Aircraft Limits*: Operate within POH/AFM; configure for low-level performance; avoid abrupt manoeuvring.
]

,homework:[
- Read Bob Tait's RAAus BAK:
  - Meteorological hazards (Pg 262–266); lost procedure (Pg 259); fatigue/body rhythms (Pg 39–40).
  - EFB/GPS limitations and requirements (v8 Theory §1.1.8).
- Prepare for next lesson (*Solo Navigation*): consolidate full manual planning; review carriage of documents (Pg 80), recency (Pg 80) and fuel requirements (Pg 89).
- Reflect: "What are the three things most likely to make a low-level leg unsafe, and how would you mitigate each?"
]
)

#show: body => lesson_plan(metadata, lesson, body)
